; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n+5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. Over 1,000 changed lines in the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) also holds. The comment shows the reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n 6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n 7. **Test.** Run `tests` (default `make test`; empty disables the step) as `/bin/sh -c` in the role's image, with no credential. On failure the agent gets the failure output in a fix step, which commits and pushes again, up to `max_test_fixes` (default 2) within the same job. A fix that changes no files, the cap, or the time running out ends the loop with the tests failed. Test logs stay on the host (kept 7 days) and are never committed.\n 8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are the role's `reviewers`, or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty (`when:` rules are ignored until #42), plus any `review:<role>` the lead deferred, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. The PR stays a draft; the merger marks it ready. A job that has pushed always gets its draft PR, even when it then ends in `ard:needs-human` or a failure, so pushed work is never left without one.\ndiff --git a/internal/committer/codepath.go b/internal/committer/codepath.go\nindex 7629d62..bc6fbbd 100644\n--- a/internal/committer/codepath.go\n+++ b/internal/committer/codepath.go\n@@ -10,7 +10,8 @@ import (\n // the one copy of the rule: the worker's diff guard (#33) and CommitCode both\n // call it. Paths must already be canonical; they are rejected, not normalised.\n //\n-// Refused: a .git segment, .gitmodules, everything under .ard/ (role, config\n+// Refused: a .git segment, .gitmodules, .gitattributes (it can collapse files\n+// out of the PR diff), everything under .ard/ (role, config\n // and issue files; the issue's own go through close-out, not through worker\n // code), everything under .ard-input/ (loop inputs), everything under the\n // root .github/ (not only workflows), and secret-shaped file names. Only the\n@@ -64,6 +65,9 @@ func CheckCodePath(issue int, p string) error {\n \tif strings.EqualFold(segs[len(segs)-1], \".gitmodules\") {\n \t\treturn reason(\"submodule configuration\")\n \t}\n+\tif strings.EqualFold(segs[len(segs)-1], \".gitattributes\") {\n+\t\treturn reason(\"attributes file (can hide changes from review)\")\n+\t}\n \tif SecretShaped(p) {\n \t\treturn reason(\"secret-shaped file name\")\n \t}\ndiff --git a/internal/committer/codepath_test.go b/internal/committer/codepath_test.go\nindex 642c6a2..365bb7a 100644\n--- a/internal/committer/codepath_test.go\n+++ b/internal/committer/codepath_test.go\n@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@ func TestCheckCodePath(t *testing.T) {\n \tr